A null pointer exception is a runtime exception in Java that occurs when you try to access or manipulate a null (non-existent) object. Null is a keyword in Java that represents the absence of any object reference.

Here's an example:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = null;
        System.out.println(str.length()); // This will throw a NullPointerException
    }
}
```

In this example, `str` is a null string. When you try to print its length, Java throws a NullPointerException because there's no string object to access.

To avoid this, you should always check if a variable is null before trying to access its properties or methods:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = null;
        if (str != null) {
            System.out.println(str.length()); // This will work
        } else {
            System.out.println("String is null");
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the if statement checks if `str` is not null before trying to access its length. If it's null, the code prints "String is null".

Remember, null is a keyword in Java, not a value. It's a special type of object that represents the absence of any object reference.
